utf8.library/Utf8Case                                    utf8.library/Utf8Case

   NAME
        Utf8Case -- Text in upper or lower case (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        length = Utf8Case(text, len, buf, size, flags)
        D0                A0    D0   A1   D1    D2

        LONG Utf8Case(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, STRPTR, LONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Writes text into buf in upper case (U8F_UPPER) or lower case
        (U8F_LOWER), every letter of every script that has case: Å, S, Zh,
        ?. Without either flag it is a plain copy, which still turns
        Latin-1 bytes into UTF-8.

   INPUTS
        text  - UTF-8 (a byte that is not UTF-8 is Latin-1)
        len   - its length in bytes, or -1: up to its 0
        buf   - where to write: UTF-8 and a 0; NULL to measure only
        size  - buf's size in bytes
        flags - U8F_UPPER or U8F_LOWER

   RESULT
        length - the bytes the whole result needs, without the 0

        Notes: One letter becomes one letter: ß stays ß in upper case
        (Unicode's SS is two letters), and the Turkish dotted and dotless
        i follow the general rule. A result that does not fit stops at the
        last whole character that does, with its 0 after it; a result
        longer than size - 1 (length >= size) was cut.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Upper](#utf8upper), Utf8Lower, Utf8Compare


utf8.library/Utf8Charset                              utf8.library/Utf8Charset

   NAME
        Utf8Charset -- A character set by its name (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        cs = Utf8Charset(name)
        D0               A0

        LONG Utf8Charset(CONST_STRPTR);

   FUNCTION
        Finds the U8CS_ number of a set from its name, as files, mail
        headers, web pages and FTP servers give it. Case, '-', '_' and
        spaces do not matter: "ISO-8859-1", "iso_8859_1" and "Latin1" are
        the same.

   INPUTS
        name - the set's name

   RESULT
        cs - U8CS_..., or -1 (not a set the library knows)

        Notes: The names known: ISO-8859-1 latin1 l1 amiga iso-ir-100
        cp819; ISO-8859-2 latin2 l2 iso-ir-101; ISO-8859-15 latin9 l9
        latin0; ISO-8859-5 cyrillic iso-ir-144; ISO-8859-7 greek
        iso-ir-126 elot928; ISO-8859-9 latin5 l5 turkish iso-ir-148;
        windows-1250 cp1250; windows-1251 cp1251; windows-1252 cp1252;
        IBM437 cp437 437 pc8 dos; IBM850 cp850 850; IBM866 cp866 866;
        KOI8-R koi8; macintosh macroman mac; US-ASCII ascii us
        ansix3.41968 iso646us; UTF-8 utf8. Since UTF-8 has a number too
        (U8CS_UTF8), Utf8FromCharset(..., Utf8Charset(name)) works for
        every name, UTF-8 included.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8CharsetName](#utf8charsetname), Utf8ToCharset, Utf8FromCharset


utf8.library/Utf8CharsetName                      utf8.library/Utf8CharsetName

   NAME
        Utf8CharsetName -- A character set's name (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        name = Utf8CharsetName(cs)
        D0                     D0

        CONST_STRPTR Utf8CharsetName(LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns the usual name of set cs, as it is written in files and
        mail ("ISO-8859-2", "windows-1252", "IBM437").

   INPUTS
        cs - U8CS_...

   RESULT
        name - the name (the library's own: do not change it), or NULL past
               the last set

        Notes: To list every set (for a menu): for (cs = 0; (name =
        Utf8CharsetName(cs)); cs++) ...

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Charset](#utf8charset)


utf8.library/Utf8CharWidth                          utf8.library/Utf8CharWidth

   NAME
        Utf8CharWidth -- How many columns a character takes (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        columns = Utf8CharWidth(cp)
        D0                      D0

        LONG Utf8CharWidth(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns how many columns of a terminal (or of any fixed-width
        text) the character takes, from Unicode 16's East Asian Width:
        Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and most emoji take two,
        accents and other marks that join the character before take none.

   INPUTS
        cp - a code point

   RESULT
        columns - 2 wide, 1, 0 (joins the one before), or -1: a control
                  (0-31, 127-159), a surrogate or not a code point

        Notes: "Ambiguous" characters (Greek and Cyrillic in East Asian
        fonts, some symbols) are 1, as in western terminals. For the width
        of a whole text use Utf8Width(), which also knows that a flag's
        two halves and an emoji sequence are one character.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Width](#utf8width), Utf8Fit, Utf8Class


utf8.library/Utf8Check                                  utf8.library/Utf8Check

   NAME
        Utf8Check -- What kind of text it is (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        kind = Utf8Check(text, len)
        D0              A0    D0

        LONG Utf8Check(CONST_STRPTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Tells plain ASCII, UTF-8 and 8-bit text (Latin-1 or another set)
        apart, for a program that has to guess what a file holds.

   INPUTS
        text - the bytes
        len  - their length, or -1: up to the 0

   RESULT
        kind - U8_ASCII:  only bytes below 128 (the same in every set)
               U8_UTF8:   good UTF-8 with characters beyond ASCII
               U8_LATIN1: has bytes that are not UTF-8, so 8-bit text

        Notes: 8-bit text that happens to be good UTF-8 is rare in
        practice: two or more accented letters in a row that form a UTF-8
        sequence. When reading a file in pieces, a sequence cut at the end
        of a piece is "not UTF-8": Utf8Valid() tells where it starts, so
        the piece can end there.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Valid](#utf8valid), Utf8FromCharset


utf8.library/Utf8Class                                  utf8.library/Utf8Class

   NAME
        Utf8Class -- What a character is (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        bits = Utf8Class(cp)
        D0               D0

        ULONG Utf8Class(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Tells what kind of character cp is: for word-wise cursor moves and
        double-click selection (UC_WORD), checking input, or drawing. One
        of the kind bits is set, and the others where they apply.

   INPUTS
        cp - a code point

   RESULT
        bits - one of
                 UC_LETTER   a letter of any script (also Chinese characters)
                 UC_DIGIT    0-9 in any script (Arabic, Devanagari, fullwidth...)
                 UC_NUMBER   other numbers: ½ ² ?
                 UC_PUNCT    punctuation and symbols, emoji too
                 UC_SPACE    spaces (also tab and line ends, with UC_CONTROL)
                 UC_MARK     accents and other marks that join the one before
                 UC_CONTROL  controls, formats, private use, unassigned
               and
                 UC_UPPER    a letter with a lower case form
                 UC_LOWER    a letter with an upper case form
                 UC_WIDE     two columns
               0 for a surrogate or past 0x10FFFF

        Notes: UC_WORD is UC_LETTER | UC_DIGIT | UC_NUMBER | UC_MARK: the
        characters that make up a word. Above U+FFFF the kind is a coarse
        one (emoji are UC_PUNCT, the historic scripts UC_LETTER, their
        marks UC_MARK).

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8CharWidth](#utf8charwidth), Utf8Upper


utf8.library/Utf8Compare                              utf8.library/Utf8Compare

   NAME
        Utf8Compare -- Compare two texts (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        order = Utf8Compare(a, alen, b, blen, flags)
        D0                  A0 D0    A1 D1    D2

        LONG Utf8Compare(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, CONST_STRPTR, LONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Compares a and b character by character, by their Unicode numbers.
        An accented letter written as one character (å) and as a letter
        and an accent (a + ring above, as Macs write file names) are the
        same, and so is a Latin-1 byte and the same letter in UTF-8.

   INPUTS
        a, b       - the texts (UTF-8; bytes that are not UTF-8 are Latin-1)
        alen, blen - their lengths, or -1: up to the 0
        flags      - 0, or:
                     U8F_NOCASE:   upper and lower case the same (A = a,
                                   S = s = s)
                     U8F_NOACCENT: letters with and without accents the
                                   same (é = e, Å = A, L = L)

   RESULT
        order - -1: a comes first, 0: the same, 1: b comes first

        Notes: The order is Unicode's, not a language's alphabet: Swedish
        Å Ä Ö come after Z only because their numbers are higher, and
        German Ä comes after Z too (where a German dictionary puts it with
        A). Enough for sorting file names; not a dictionary sort.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Find](#utf8find), Utf8Normalize


utf8.library/Utf8Decode                                utf8.library/Utf8Decode

   NAME
        Utf8Decode -- Read one character (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        used = Utf8Decode(text, len, cp)
        D0              A0    D0   A1

        LONG Utf8Decode(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, ULONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Reads the character at text and gives its code point. The building
        block for a program's own loops over UTF-8.

   INPUTS
        text - where the character starts
        len  - the bytes there are, or -1: up to a 0 byte
        cp   - where to put the code point (may be NULL)

   RESULT
        used - 1 to 4: the character's bytes, good UTF-8
               -1: the byte is not UTF-8 (a stray byte, a sequence cut
                   short, too long a way to write a small one, a
                   surrogate); *cp is that byte as Latin-1, and the next
                   character is one byte on
                0: nothing left (len 0, or a 0 byte with len -1)

        Notes: A loop over every code point:

        while ((k = Utf8Decode(p, end - p, &c)) != 0) {
            ...                     /* c: the character */
            p += k < 0 ? 1 : k;
        }

        This gives code points. What a reader sees as one character can be
        several (a letter and an accent, a flag): Utf8Next() steps by
        those.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Encode](#utf8encode), Utf8Next, Utf8Valid


utf8.library/Utf8Encode                                utf8.library/Utf8Encode

   NAME
        Utf8Encode -- Write one character (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        bytes = Utf8Encode(cp, buf)
        D0               D0  A0

        LONG Utf8Encode(ULONG, STRPTR);

   FUNCTION
        Writes code point cp as UTF-8.

   INPUTS
        cp  - a code point, 0 to 0x10FFFF
        buf - room for 4 bytes (no 0 is written after them)

   RESULT
        bytes - 1 to 4, or 0: cp is a surrogate (0xD800-0xDFFF) or past
                0x10FFFF, and nothing was written

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Decode](#utf8decode)


utf8.library/Utf8Find                                    utf8.library/Utf8Find

   NAME
        Utf8Find -- Find a text in a text (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        pos = Utf8Find(text, len, find, flen, flags, matchlen)
        D0             A0    D0   A1    D1    D2     A2

        LONG Utf8Find(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, CONST_STRPTR, LONG, ULONG, LONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Looks for find in text, as Utf8Compare() compares: accents written
        either way are the same, and with the flags case and accents can
        be left out. "gote" finds "Göteborg" with U8F_NOCASE |
        U8F_NOACCENT.

   INPUTS
        text     - where to look (UTF-8; bytes that are not UTF-8 are Latin-1)
        len      - its length, or -1: up to the 0
        find     - what to look for
        flen     - its length, or -1
        flags    - 0, U8F_NOCASE, U8F_NOACCENT, or both
        matchlen - where to put the length in bytes of what was found (may
                   be NULL)

   RESULT
        pos - the byte where the first match starts, or -1 (not found);
              0 with *matchlen 0 when find is empty

        Notes: A match starts and ends on whole characters: "e" does not
        find the e of an é written as e + accent unless U8F_NOACCENT is
        given. The match can be longer or shorter in bytes than find ("e"
        finds "é", two bytes, with U8F_NOACCENT): use *matchlen, not flen,
        to mark or replace it. For the next match search again from pos +
        *matchlen.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Compare](#utf8compare)


utf8.library/Utf8Fit                                      utf8.library/Utf8Fit

   NAME
        Utf8Fit -- How much fits in some columns (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        bytes = Utf8Fit(text, len, cols, width)
        D0            A0    D0   D1    A1

        LONG Utf8Fit(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, LONG, LONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Finds how much of the start of text fits in cols columns: for
        cutting a line at the edge of a window, or finding which character
        a mouse click at a column lands on.

   INPUTS
        text  - UTF-8
        len   - its length, or -1: up to the 0
        cols  - the columns there are
        width - where to put the columns the part that fits takes (may be
                NULL); it can be one less than cols when a wide character
                did not fit

   RESULT
        bytes - the length of the part that fits: never half a character,
                and a character's accents always with it

        Notes: Controls count 0 columns, as in Utf8Width(). To cut a line
        with "..." at the end: Utf8Fit(text, len, cols - 3, &w), write
        those bytes and the three dots.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Width](#utf8width), Utf8CharWidth


utf8.library/Utf8FromCharset                      utf8.library/Utf8FromCharset

   NAME
        Utf8FromCharset -- 8-bit text to UTF-8 (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        length = Utf8FromCharset(text, len, buf, size, cs)
        D0                       A0    D0   A1   D1    D2

        LONG Utf8FromCharset(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, STRPTR, LONG, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Turns text in an 8-bit set into UTF-8: Amiga text (U8CS_LATIN1)
        for a web page or a mail, a PC's ANSI art (U8CS_CP437), a Polish
        file (U8CS_LATIN2).

   INPUTS
        text - the 8-bit text
        len  - its length, or -1: up to the 0
        buf  - where to write: UTF-8 and a 0; NULL to measure only
        size - buf's size in bytes
        cs   - the set the text is in: U8CS_...

   RESULT
        length - the bytes the whole result needs, without the 0; -1 when cs
                 is not a set

        Notes: A byte the set has nothing for (0x81 in windows-1252, any
        byte from 128 up in U8CS_ASCII) becomes U+FFFD, the replacement
        character. With U8CS_UTF8 the text is copied, and bytes that are
        not UTF-8 become their Latin-1 letters: good UTF-8 out whatever
        came in. A result that does not fit stops at the last whole
        character, with its 0.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8ToCharset](#utf8tocharset), Utf8Charset, Utf8Check


utf8.library/Utf8Length                                utf8.library/Utf8Length

   NAME
        Utf8Length -- How many characters (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        count = Utf8Length(text, len)
        D0                A0    D0

        LONG Utf8Length(CONST_STRPTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Counts the characters as a reader sees them: a letter with its
        accents is one (also when written as a letter and separate
        accents), a flag (two regional letters) is one, an emoji family
        joined by zero-width joiners is one, and CR LF is one.

   INPUTS
        text - UTF-8 (a byte that is not UTF-8 is one Latin-1 character)
        len  - its length in bytes, or -1: up to its 0

   RESULT
        count - the characters

        Notes: To count code points instead, loop with Utf8Decode(). The
        rules are a short form of Unicode's grapheme clusters (UAX #29):
        marks, joiners, variation selectors, skin tones, tags and Hangul
        vowels join the character before; Indic conjuncts are not put
        together.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Next](#utf8next), Utf8Width


utf8.library/Utf8Lower                                  utf8.library/Utf8Lower

   NAME
        Utf8Lower -- A character in lower case (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        lower = Utf8Lower(cp)
        D0                D0

        ULONG Utf8Lower(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns the lower case form of a character: Å gives å, S gives s,
        Zh gives zh.

   INPUTS
        cp - a code point

   RESULT
        lower - its lower case form, or cp itself (no case, or already lower)

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Upper](#utf8upper), Utf8Case, Utf8Class


utf8.library/Utf8Next                                    utf8.library/Utf8Next

   NAME
        Utf8Next -- To the next character (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        next = Utf8Next(text, len, pos)
        D0              A0    D0   D1

        LONG Utf8Next(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Moves from the character at byte pos to the start of the next one,
        as a reader counts them (see Utf8Length): what an editor's
        cursor-right does.

   INPUTS
        text - UTF-8
        len  - its length, or -1: up to the 0
        pos  - where a character starts

   RESULT
        next - where the next starts; len at the end (and for pos >= len)

        Notes: pos should be where a character starts (0, or what Utf8Next
        or Utf8Prev gave). From the middle of one it moves a byte at a
        time.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Prev](#utf8prev), Utf8Length


utf8.library/Utf8NoAccent                            utf8.library/Utf8NoAccent

   NAME
        Utf8NoAccent -- A letter without its accents (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        plain = Utf8NoAccent(cp)
        D0                   D0

        ULONG Utf8NoAccent(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns the letter without accents: é gives e, Å gives A, e (two
        accents) gives e, Y gives I, ga gives ka. Letters with a stroke that
        Unicode does not take apart are covered too: Ø, L, D, H, T, i and
        a few more give O, L, D, H, T, i.

   INPUTS
        cp - a code point

   RESULT
        plain - the letter without accents, or cp itself

        Notes: Covers Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, Japanese kana and the signs
        O, K, Å that are other letters in disguise. Letters that are not a
        letter with an accent stay as they are: Æ, ß, Þ (Utf8ToCharset
        writes AE, ss for the first two when a set lacks them).

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Find](#utf8find), Utf8Compare, Utf8ToCharset


utf8.library/Utf8Normalize                          utf8.library/Utf8Normalize

   NAME
        Utf8Normalize -- Accents together or apart (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        length = Utf8Normalize(text, len, buf, size, flags)
        D0                     A0    D0   A1   D1    D2

        LONG Utf8Normalize(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, STRPTR, LONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Writes text with every accent put on its letter (U8F_COMPOSE: å as
        one character, as Amigas, Windows and the web write it) or taken
        off as a separate mark (U8F_DECOMPOSE: a + ring above, as Macs may
        write file names). Use it on names from a Mac (over NFS or SMB)
        before showing or comparing them.

   INPUTS
        text  - UTF-8 (a byte that is not UTF-8 is Latin-1)
        len   - its length, or -1: up to its 0
        buf   - where to write: UTF-8 and a 0; NULL to measure only
        size  - buf's size in bytes
        flags - U8F_COMPOSE or U8F_DECOMPOSE (0: a plain copy that turns
                Latin-1 bytes into UTF-8)

   RESULT
        length - the bytes the whole result needs, without the 0

        Notes: For Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, kana and Hangul it gives what
        Unicode's NFC and NFD give (checked against Python's for every
        character there). Other scripts pass through unchanged, and marks
        are not put in Unicode's order when several are on one letter in
        an unusual order.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Compare](#utf8compare), Utf8ToCharset


utf8.library/Utf8Prev                                    utf8.library/Utf8Prev

   NAME
        Utf8Prev -- To the character before (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        prev = Utf8Prev(text, len, pos)
        D0              A0    D0   D1

        LONG Utf8Prev(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Moves from byte pos to the start of the character before it: what
        an editor's cursor-left and backspace need. Accents, flags and
        emoji sequences are passed over whole.

   INPUTS
        text - UTF-8
        len  - its length, or -1: up to the 0
        pos  - a position in the text

   RESULT
        prev - where the character before pos starts; 0 at the start. From
               the middle of a character: where that one starts

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Next](#utf8next)


utf8.library/Utf8ToCharset                          utf8.library/Utf8ToCharset

   NAME
        Utf8ToCharset -- UTF-8 to an 8-bit set (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        length = Utf8ToCharset(text, len, buf, size, cs, flags)
        D0                     A0    D0   A1   D1    D2  D3

        LONG Utf8ToCharset(CONST_STRPTR, LONG, STRPTR, LONG, LONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Turns UTF-8 into an 8-bit set: Latin-1 for an Amiga screen, a
        console window or a file other Amiga programs read; Latin-2 for a
        Polish Amiga; CP437 for a PC BBS. Accents written as separate
        marks are put on their letters first, so a Mac's "a + ring" still
        becomes Latin-1's å.

   INPUTS
        text  - UTF-8 (a byte that is not UTF-8 is Latin-1)
        len   - its length, or -1: up to its 0
        buf   - where to write: the set's bytes and a 0; NULL to measure
        size  - buf's size in bytes
        cs    - the set: U8CS_...
        flags - 0: the nearest for what the set lacks; U8F_STRICT: '?'

   RESULT
        length - the bytes the whole result needs, without the 0; -1 when cs
                 is not a set

        Notes: Without U8F_STRICT, what the set lacks becomes the nearest
        it has: the letter without its accent (L gives L in Latin-1),
        typographic quotes and dashes give " ' -, ... gives ..., EUR gives EUR
        (in sets without EUR), TM gives TM, <= gives <=, box lines give - | +,
        a full block #, a fullwidth A an A. Cyrillic, Greek, Japanese kana
        and Korean are spelt in Latin letters (0.1b2): Privet gives
        Privet, Omega Omega, konnichiha konnichiha, seoul seoul; small kana join
        the kana before (kya kya). What has nothing near (Chinese
        characters, kanji, emoji) gives '?'; a flag gives its two letters.
        Invisible characters (zero-width joiners, variation selectors, a
        byte order mark, skin tones) give nothing. One character can give
        up to seven bytes (a Korean syllable), so the result can be longer
        than the text. A result that does not fit stops at a whole
        character, with its 0, and is always the start of the whole
        result.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8FromCharset](#utf8fromcharset), Utf8Charset, Utf8NoAccent


utf8.library/Utf8Upper                                  utf8.library/Utf8Upper

   NAME
        Utf8Upper -- A character in upper case (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        upper = Utf8Upper(cp)
        D0                D0

        ULONG Utf8Upper(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns the upper case form of a character: å gives Å, s and s
        give S, ÿ gives Y.

   INPUTS
        cp - a code point

   RESULT
        upper - its upper case form, or cp itself (no case, already upper, or
                no single-letter upper case: ß)

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Lower](#utf8lower), Utf8Case, Utf8Class


utf8.library/Utf8Valid                                  utf8.library/Utf8Valid

   NAME
        Utf8Valid -- Where the text stops being UTF-8 (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        pos = Utf8Valid(text, len)
        D0             A0    D0

        LONG Utf8Valid(CONST_STRPTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Checks that text is good UTF-8 (RFC 3629: no stray bytes, nothing
        cut short, no needlessly long forms, no surrogates, nothing past
        U+10FFFF).

   INPUTS
        text - the bytes
        len  - their length, or -1: up to the 0

   RESULT
        pos - -1: all good; else the byte where the first fault starts

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Check](#utf8check), Utf8Decode


utf8.library/Utf8Width                                  utf8.library/Utf8Width

   NAME
        Utf8Width -- How many columns a text takes (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        columns = Utf8Width(text, len)
        D0                 A0    D0

        LONG Utf8Width(CONST_STRPTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Returns how many columns of a terminal the text takes: ?? takes 4,
        é written as e + accent takes 1, a flag takes 2. For laying out
        columns, centring a title, or knowing whether a line fits.

   INPUTS
        text - UTF-8 (a byte that is not UTF-8 is a Latin-1 character, 1 column)
        len  - its length, or -1: up to the 0

   RESULT
        columns - the columns

        Notes: Controls (tab, line ends, escape) count 0: where a tab goes
        is the program's business. A character takes its first code
        point's width; a flag takes 2. Emoji with a variation selector
        (U+FE0F) after a normally narrow symbol are counted 1, as most
        terminals do.

   SEE ALSO
        Utf8Fit](#utf8fit), Utf8CharWidth

